<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chota Nagpur Plateau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chota Nagpur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chota <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> Plateau is an upland of forests and minerals in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, covering most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. It is home to Adivasi peoples such as the Munda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukh-people">Oraon</a>. Under British rule its small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, including Surguja, Jashpur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, formed the Chota Nagpur Tributary States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau.jpg" alt="Chota Nagpur Plateau" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chota_Nagpur_Plateau-IGI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
